why nativeEvent in Safari browswer doesn't support isComposing ?

Safari's implementation of isComposing on native events has had inconsistent support compared to other browsers. Here's what's happening:

The Issue
The isComposing property is part of the InputEvent and CompositionEvent interfaces, indicating whether the event is fired during a composition session (like when typing with an IME - Input Method Editor for languages like Chinese, Japanese, or Korean).

Safari has historically had incomplete or buggy implementation of this property:

Older Safari versions (before Safari 10.1) didn't support isComposing at all
Some Safari versions return undefined instead of false when not composing
Event type differences - Safari might not include isComposing on all event types where other browsers do
Workarounds

Here are some approaches to handle this:

1. Feature Detection with Fallback
javascript
function isComposing(event) {
  // Check if isComposing exists and use it
  if ('isComposing' in event) {
    return event.isComposing;
  }
  
  // Fallback: check if we're in a composition session
  // using compositionstart/compositionend events
  return isInCompositionSession;
}

// Track composition state manually
let isInCompositionSession = false;

element.addEventListener('compositionstart', () => {
  isInCompositionSession = true;
});

element.addEventListener('compositionend', () => {
  isInCompositionSession = false;
});

2. Use Composition Events Directly
javascript
let composing = false;

input.addEventListener('compositionstart', () => {
  composing = true;
});

input.addEventListener('compositionend', () => {
  composing = false;
  // Handle the final input after composition
});

input.addEventListener('input', (e) => {
  if (!composing) {
    // Safe to process input
  }
});

3. Polyfill Approach
javascript
// Add isComposing to events if missing
if (!('isComposing' in InputEvent.prototype)) {
  let composing = false;
  
  document.addEventListener('compositionstart', () => {
    composing = true;
  }, true);
  
  document.addEventListener('compositionend', () => {
    composing = false;
  }, true);
  
  const originalAddEventListener = EventTarget.prototype.addEventListener;
  EventTarget.prototype.addEventListener = function(type, listener, options) {
    if (type === 'input' || type === 'keydown' || type === 'keyup') {
      const wrappedListener = function(event) {
        if (!('isComposing' in event)) {
          Object.defineProperty(event, 'isComposing', {
            get: () => composing,
            configurable: true
          });
        }
        listener.call(this, event);
      };
      return originalAddEventListener.call(this, type, wrappedListener, options);
    }
    return originalAddEventListener.call(this, type, listener, options);
  };
}
Modern Safari Support
Recent versions of Safari (14+) have improved support for isComposing, but you should still implement fallbacks for:

Older Safari versions
Safari on older iOS devices
Edge cases where the property might not be available
The most reliable cross-browser approach is to use the composition events (compositionstart, compositionend) to track the composition state manually rather than relying solely on isComposing.


i saw a workaround like this 'event.nativeEvent.keyCode === 229'
is it an appropriate approach?

Yes, checking keyCode === 229 is a valid workaround, but it comes with important caveats. Let me explain:

What is keyCode 229?
When an IME (Input Method Editor) is active and processing input, browsers often report keyCode as 229. This is sometimes called the "process" key code, indicating that the key event is being processed by the IME.

javascript
input.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {
  if (e.keyCode === 229) {
    console.log('IME is processing input');
  }
});

Why It Works
This approach works because:

Most browsers consistently use 229 during IME composition
It's been around longer than isComposing, so it works in older browsers
Safari reliably reports this keyCode during composition
Limitations and Issues

1. Not Always Reliable
javascript
// This might miss some composition scenarios
if (e.keyCode === 229) {
  // Might be composing, but not guaranteed
  // Some IMEs or scenarios might not trigger 229
}

2. Deprecated API
javascript
// keyCode is deprecated in favor of key and code
element.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {
  // ❌ Deprecated
  if (e.keyCode === 229) { }
  
  // ✅ Modern approach (though less reliable for IME detection)
  if (e.key === 'Process') { }
});

3. False Positives/Negatives
Some non-IME scenarios might produce keyCode 229
Some IME inputs might not produce keyCode 229
Mobile browsers can be inconsistent
Better Combined Approach
Here's a more robust solution combining multiple methods:

javascript
class CompositionTracker {
  constructor() {
    this.isComposing = false;
    this.keyCode229Detected = false;
  }

  init(element) {
    // Track via composition events (most reliable)
    element.addEventListener('compositionstart', () => {
      this.isComposing = true;
    });

    element.addEventListener('compositionend', () => {
      this.isComposing = false;
      this.keyCode229Detected = false;
    });

    // Track via keyCode 229 (fallback for Safari)
    element.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {
      if (e.keyCode === 229 || e.key === 'Process') {
        this.keyCode229Detected = true;
      }
    });

    element.addEventListener('keyup', (e) => {
      if (e.keyCode !== 229 && e.key !== 'Process') {
        // If we get a non-229 keyup, composition might be ending
        this.keyCode229Detected = false;
      }
    });
  }

  isInComposition(event) {
    // Check multiple indicators
    return (
      // Modern browsers
      (event.isComposing === true) ||
      // Composition events tracking
      this.isComposing ||
      // Safari fallback
      (event.keyCode === 229) ||
      (event.key === 'Process') ||
      // Our tracked state
      this.keyCode229Detected
    );
  }
}

// Usage
const tracker = new CompositionTracker();
tracker.init(inputElement);

inputElement.addEventListener('input', (e) => {
  if (!tracker.isInComposition(e)) {
    // Safe to process input
    handleInput(e);
  }
});
Recommendation
The keyCode === 229 check is appropriate as a fallback, especially for Safari, but shouldn't be your only method because:

Use it in combination with composition events and isComposing when available
Be aware it's deprecated - modern code should also check e.key === 'Process'
Test thoroughly with the specific IMEs your users use (Chinese, Japanese, Korean, etc.)
Consider the context - for critical applications, use multiple detection methods
For Safari specifically, the combination of keyCode === 229 detection with composition events gives you the best coverage across different versions and scenarios.
